This block states the required interface identity, resolution, invocation, input shape, and setup that the implementation must realize. It does not state expected outcomes, prescribe private implementation structure, or expand edit scope.

```text
test_interface = compiled/library (signals: ['"'"'compile-from-source'"'"', '"'"'link-symbol'"'"', '"'"'shipped-cli'"'"', '"'"'subprocess-harness'"'"'])

This block states only where and how existing callers reach the implementation.
It does not state expected outcomes or prescribe implementation structure.

## Reached at
  The implementation is a C project tree (libming) rooted at /app, staged and
  built by the image build. All callers address it by absolute path only; the
  caller strips the environment down to a bare PATH and HOME and passes every
  file by absolute path, so no environment variable, PATH lookup, or working
  directory mediates the reach.

  Primary transport — compile from source at every run. The caller compiles
  four of the project'"'"'s own source files by absolute path into /tmp/vsb_build,
  links each against a small caller-owned main, and runs the resulting binary:
      gcc … -I /app/util -c /app/util/read.c        -o /tmp/vsb_build/read.o
      gcc … -I /app/util/old -c /app/util/old/read.c -o /tmp/vsb_build/oldread.o
      gcc … -I /app/src -I /app/src/blocks -I /app/util \
            -Dmain=vsb_app_main -c /app/util/listfdb.c -o /tmp/vsb_build/listfdb.o
      gcc … -I /app/src -I /app/src/blocks -c /app/src/blocks/input.c \
            -o /tmp/vsb_build/input.o
  Because the objects are rebuilt from the current sources before every run,
  only the live source text under /app is exercised.

  Secondary transport — shipped CLI executables, run by absolute path:
      /app/util/listswf  <file>
      /app/util/listfdb  <file>
  These are artifacts of the project'"'"'s own autotools build (util/Makefile.am:
  listswf from util/main.c plus the util parser library; listfdb from
  util/listfdb.c). They are project-owned, not system tools.

## Must provide
  Source files — each must exist and compile as a standalone translation unit:
    /app/util/read.c          defines readRect(FILE *, struct Rect *),
                              readSBits(FILE *, int), readBits(FILE *, int),
                              byteAlign(); includes "parser.h"; compiled with
                              -I /app/util
    /app/util/old/read.c      defines readSBits(FILE *, int),
                              readBits(FILE *, int), byteAlign(); includes
                              "read.h"; compiled with -I /app/util/old
    /app/util/listfdb.c       defines main(int argc, char **argv) plus the
                              file-scope readers that main uses; includes
                              "blocks/blocktypes.h" and "action.h"; compiled
                              with -I /app/src -I /app/src/blocks -I /app/util
                              and -Dmain=vsb_app_main, so the file must keep a
                              real main(int, char **)
    /app/src/blocks/input.c   defines SWFInput_readSBits(SWFInput, int) and
                              newSWFInput_buffer(unsigned char *, int)
                              (SWFInput is struct SWFInput_s *); compiled with
                              -I /app/src -I /app/src/blocks
  Headers the caller'"'"'s harnesses include by name:
    /app/util/parser.h        must declare struct Rect with the four members
                              int xMin, xMax, yMin, yMax — the harness reads
                              and prints through exactly these member names
    /app/util/read.h          declares the util/read.c entry points above
    /app/util/old/read.h      declares the util/old/read.c entry points above
  Shipped executables, present and runnable by absolute path, each taking one
  positional file-path argument:
    /app/util/listswf
    /app/util/listfdb

## Called as
  Harness binary over /app/util/read.c (built at run time at
  /tmp/vsb_build/readrect):
      readrect rect <swf-file>
      argv[1] — selector string; argv[2] — a file-path string. The caller'"'"'s
      main opens argv[2] and positions the FILE* eight bytes from the start of
      the file, then calls:
          readRect(f, &r)
      f — an open FILE* at that position; &r — a pointer to a struct Rect.

  Harness binary over /app/util/listfdb.c (built at run time at
  /tmp/vsb_build/lfdt):
      lfdt app <fdb-file>
      argv[1] — selector string; argv[2] — a file-path string. The caller'"'"'s
      main forwards to the renamed application entry point:
          vsb_app_main(argc - 1, argv + 1)
      so the file'"'"'s own main runs with argc == 2 and the file path in argv[1].

  Harness binary over /app/src/blocks/input.c (built at run time at
  /tmp/vsb_build/inread):
      inread val <bits-file> <count>
      argv[1] — selector string; argv[2] — a file-path string; argv[3] — a
      string. The caller'"'"'s main reads up to 256 bytes of argv[2] into a byte
      buffer, then calls:
          newSWFInput_buffer(buf, n)
      buf — unsigned char * over the bytes read; n — int byte count. Then:
          SWFInput_readSBits(in, atoi(argv[3]))
      in — the SWFInput from the call above; second argument — an int
      obtained from argv[3] via atoi.

  Harness binary over /app/util/old/read.c (built at run time at
  /tmp/vsb_build/oldrect):
      oldrect rect <bits-file>
      argv[1] — selector string; argv[2] — a file-path string. The caller'"'"'s
      main opens argv[2], then calls, against that FILE*:
          byteAlign();
          n = readBits(f, 5);
          readSBits(f, n);   /* called four times with the same n */

  Shipped executables:
      /app/util/listswf <swf-file>    one positional argument; a file-path
                                      string
      /app/util/listfdb <fdb-file>    one positional argument; a file-path
                                      string

  All input files are small binary files (SWF-shaped, FDB-shaped, or raw bit
  streams) created by the caller under /tmp/vsb_build and passed by absolute
  path.

## Setup required
  - Each of the four source files must compile as one standalone translation
    unit under the include paths (and, for /app/util/listfdb.c only, the
    -Dmain=vsb_app_main definition) listed above, using /usr/bin/gcc, and the
    caller compiles and links with a definition that expands every
    __attribute__(...) annotation away — the sources must still compile under
    that.
  - Link budget: each object is linked only with the caller'"'"'s own main (and
    for /app/src/blocks/input.c, one caller-owned stub object). Beyond the C
    library, the caller supplies link-time definitions for exactly four
    symbols: _SWF_warn, _SWF_error, ming_gc_add_node, ming_gc_remove_node.
    Any further external reference in a translation unit has nothing to
    resolve against.
  - Nothing is found via PATH, working directory, or environment; every
    source, header, binary, and input file is addressed by absolute path and
    the caller runs with a stripped environment (bare PATH and HOME).
  - The system toolchain (/usr/bin/gcc, cc1, as, ld, the C library, the
    dynamic loader, and the runtime libraries the caller'"'"'s flags use) is
    preexisting and is pinned by the caller at run time; it is not part of the
    implementation.

## Test harness boundary
  A pytest runner drives everything through subprocess: it performs the
  compiles and links above at run time, materializes every input file under
  /tmp/vsb_build itself, and runs each binary with the argv shapes listed
  above. The following belong to the calling side and must not be rebuilt or
  modified as part of the implementation: the four small wrapper mains linked
  against the project objects, the stub object linked with input.c, a probe
  function that is force-included into each translation unit under test (each
  harness binary also accepts a selector subcommand that reaches only this
  caller-owned probe, never project code), all input files, and the toolchain
  integrity pinning. The project-owned side is exactly: the four source files
  under /app named above with the headers they include, and the two shipped
  executables /app/util/listswf and /app/util/listfdb.
```

