  - `public function __construct($data=array())` — callable with zero arguments. Retained constructors: `new CDbCriteria()` (validators, AR defaults) and `new CDbCriteria($array)` (command builder `createCriteria`, AR relation options, data provider). Also reachable from within the class via the retained `mergeWith`, which builds a criteria from an array argument.
  - `public function __wakeup()` — public, zero parameters, no return value. Invoked by the PHP runtime itself during `unserialize()` of a serialized `CDbCriteria`, and invoked directly on live instances by retained callers.
- **Observable behavior:** The property declares the criteria'"'"'s scope slot. The constructor initializes a new instance, optionally from a map of initial property values. `__wakeup()` re-keys the criteria'"'"'s auto-generated query parameters after an instance is restored (or on demand), so restored criteria keep generating names that continue from the shared counter instead of reusing names already consumed before serialization.
- **Inputs:** `$data`: array of property-name => value pairs (callers pass any subset of the class'"'"'s public property names; may be omitted). `__wakeup()` takes none; it reads the instance'"'"'s `params` and the six clause fields `select`, `condition`, `order`, `group`, `join`, `having`, plus the class'"'"'s `PARAM_PREFIX` constant and static `$paramCount`.
- **Outputs and errors:** Constructor returns the initialized instance; each `$data` entry is assigned to the same-named public property, other properties keep their declared defaults. `__wakeup()` mutates the instance in place and returns nothing.
- **Side effects and interactions:** `__wakeup()` reads and increments the shared static counter `CDbCriteria::$paramCount`; retained code positions that counter before serializing and before restoring, so names it generates interleave with externally generated `PARAM_PREFIX`-based names. Constructor writes only the instance.
- **Required sequencing and state:** `__wakeup()`: the rebuilt map takes the entries of `params` in order: an entry whose key starts with `PARAM_PREFIX` is moved to a fresh key — the prefix plus the counter'"'"'s current value, then the counter increments — all other keys are kept, every value preserved in order under its (possibly new) key. When at least one key was renamed, every occurrence of each old name inside the six clause fields is replaced by its new name, element by element when a field holds an array, across the field'"'"'s value when it holds a string; when none was renamed, those fields are untouched. Finally `params` is replaced with the rebuilt map. Repeated calls re-key again from the counter'"'"'s then-current value. Non-prefixed keys keep their names across repeated round trips; `distinct`, `limit`, `offset`, `with`, `alias`, `together`, `index`, `scopes` pass through unchanged.
- **Region boundary:** Not self-contained (mechanical consequence of where the range was cut). The retained text above ends at the `public $scopes;` statement inside class `CDbCriteria extends CComponent`: the replacement must not open the class or re-declare `scopes` — it begins at class-body level, after that statement. The retained text below is `$this->params=$params;`, then `}` closing the method and `}` closing the class: the replacement must end inside `__wakeup()` with the rebuilt map bound to a local named `$params` — the retained assignment line consumes that name, which must therefore still be in scope at the region'"'"'s last line — and must leave the method body unclosed. It must fully close `__construct()`.
