  and analogously for every other route listed below. Request inputs arrive as
  ordinary named request parameters; callers place them in the form-encoded
  POST body. The routing key s itself sits in the URL query string.
  A real call as written by callers:
      session.post("http://localhost/server/index.php?s=/api/user/updateInfo",
                   data={"name": <string>}, timeout=30)

## Must provide
  Every route below is resolved by the framework to a public action method of the
  named controller class, all under
  /var/www/html/server/Application/Api/Controller/ . All controllers extend the
  shared BaseController in the same directory; the ThinkPHP dispatcher that maps
  the s parameter to module/controller/action ships inside the staged tree at
  /var/www/html/server/ThinkPHP and is a runtime prerequisite, not caller code.

    /api/user/register         POST        UserController::register()
    /api/user/login            POST        UserController::login()
    /api/user/info             GET         UserController::info()
    /api/user/updateInfo       GET, POST   UserController::updateInfo()
    /api/user/logout           GET, POST   UserController::logout()
    /api/team/save             POST        TeamController::save()
    /api/teamMember/save       GET, POST   TeamMemberController::save()
    /api/teamMember/getList    POST        TeamMemberController::getList()
    /api/item/add              POST        ItemController::add()
    /api/member/save           GET, POST   MemberController::save()
    /api/member/getList        POST        MemberController::getList()
    /api/member/delete         GET, POST   MemberController::delete()

## Called as
  All calls go to http://localhost/server/index.php through a persistent HTTP
  session whose cookie carries the caller'"'"'s authenticated identity, established
  first through /api/user/register and /api/user/login. Every state-changing call
  is issued under that authenticated session. Inputs are named parameters.

  POST calls send application/x-www-form-urlencoded body fields:

    /api/user/register        username, password, confirm_password — each a string
    /api/user/login           username, password — each a string
    /api/user/updateInfo      name — a string
    /api/user/logout          confirm — a number
    /api/team/save            team_name — a string
    /api/item/add             item_name — a string
    /api/teamMember/save      team_id — a number or a string;
                              member_username — a string
    /api/teamMember/getList   team_id — a number or a string
    /api/member/save          item_id — a number or a string; username — a string;
                              member_group_id — a number or a string
    /api/member/getList       item_id — a number or a string
    /api/member/delete        item_id — a number or a string;
                              item_member_id — a number or a string

  GET calls to the same five state-changing routes also occur and are answered.
  Those requests carry, alongside s, query-string keys using the same names, e.g.
      http://localhost/server/index.php?s=/api/user/updateInfo&name=<string>
      http://localhost/server/index.php?s=/api/teamMember/save&team_id=<value>&member_username=<string>
      http://localhost/server/index.php?s=/api/member/save&item_id=<value>&username=<string>&member_group_id=<value>
      http://localhost/server/index.php?s=/api/member/delete&item_id=<value>&item_member_id=<value>
  and one route receives a bare GET with no parameters beyond s:
      http://localhost/server/index.php?s=/api/user/logout
  /api/user/info is reached by a plain GET with no parameters beyond s.

## Setup required
  - The Apache server must already be serving the application before callers
    proceed; callers poll http://localhost/server/index.php until it answers,
    then make their calls.
  - The application is the project tree staged by the image build at
    /var/www/html: the entry script, the Api module controller classes listed
    above, the bundled ThinkPHP framework, and the composer-installed vendor/
    autoloader. It is project-owned code, not a system package or a prebuilt
    binary.
  - The image build enables Apache mod_rewrite and AllowOverride for the
    document root, installs the pdo_sqlite and zip PHP extensions, and sets a
    configuration value in server/Application/Api/Conf/config.php so that
    register and login proceed without a verification code; callers depend on
    that configuration staying in effect.
  - Application data lives in the SQLite database at
    /var/www/html/Sqlite/showdoc.db.php, writable by the web-server user; the
    directory server/Application/Runtime must stay writable as well.
  - The records a caller manages in one request were created by the same session
    in earlier requests: teams via /api/team/save and projects via
    /api/item/add, so identity, ownership and membership state must carry across
    requests within one session.

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side is a Python pytest runner executing inside the same container
  as the Apache/PHP application. It uses the requests library to issue the HTTP
  calls above, keeps one cookie session per scenario, and generates fresh
  usernames and field values at runtime. A shell readiness loop polls the server
  with curl before the runner starts. None of this belongs to the implementation:
  the runner, its session bookkeeping, its generated values, and the readiness
  poller are caller-side machinery and must not be rebuilt.
