## Reached at
  A browser-side JavaScript library served as static files from the `/app` tree,
  staged there by the image build (WORKDIR `/app`). The runtime is a
  Playwright-driven headless Chromium. The library is loaded with a plain script
  tag, both from documents served on the app origin and from documents on other
  origins:

      <script src="sysend.js"></script>
      <script src="<app origin>/sysend.js"></script>

  In the browser the module'"'"'s UMD wrapper assigns the library to `window.sysend`;
  callers resolve it as that global after page load. Documents callers navigate to:

      http://localhost:8080/demo.html    shipped demo page; loads sysend.js
      <app origin>/proxy.html            shipped proxy document; loads sysend.js

  The retained package.json declares name `sysend` with main `sysend.js`.

  Cross-origin relay surface: an app page calls `sysend.proxy(url)` with the URL
  of a proxy document that lives on another origin, and that document loads this
  same library on its own origin. The app'"'"'s own `/proxy.html` is likewise
  embedded as an iframe by pages on other origins; inside such an iframe the
  library runs in the app'"'"'s origin, and window message events posted to the
  iframe'"'"'s contentWindow reach a `message` listener the library registers for
  embedded instances.

## Must provide
  /app/sysend.js       static JS file; loaded as a script it assigns window.sysend
                       and initializes itself at load time (callers never call an
                       init function)
  /app/demo.html       shipped page that loads sysend.js; callers navigate to
                       /demo.html and drive the global from it; its own retained
                       script also calls into the library at page load
  /app/proxy.html      shipped proxy document at path /proxy.html on the app
                       origin; loads sysend.js through a relative script tag and
                       is embedded as an iframe by pages on other origins
  sysend.broadcast     function on the window.sysend object
  sysend.emit          function on the window.sysend object
  sysend.on            function on the window.sysend object
  sysend.off           function on the window.sysend object
  sysend.proxy         function on the window.sysend object
  sysend.isPrimary     function on the window.sysend object
  sysend.id            own property on window.sysend; callers depend on a
                       non-empty string here
  sysend.track         function; called by the shipped demo page'"'"'s own script
  sysend.untrack       function; called by the shipped demo page'"'"'s own script
  sysend.list          function; called by the shipped demo page'"'"'s own script
  sysend.post          function; called by the shipped demo page'"'"'s own script

## Called as
  Calls are made in page context, as inline JavaScript evaluated on pages served
  from the app origin:

  sysend.on(event, callback)        event — 1st positional argument; a string
                                          naming a channel event
                                    callback — 2nd positional argument; a function
  sysend.emit(event, data)          event — 1st positional argument; a string
                                    data — 2nd positional argument; a
                                          JSON-serializable value; callers pass
                                          plain objects with string, number,
                                          boolean, null, array, and nested-object
                                          members, and the empty object
  sysend.broadcast(event, data)     same positions and shapes as emit; called in
                                          one tab while a listener registered with
                                          sysend.on exists in another tab of the
                                          same origin
  sysend.off(event, callback)       event — 1st positional argument; a string
                                    callback — 2nd positional argument; the same
                                          function reference earlier passed to
                                          sysend.on
  sysend.proxy(url)                 url — 1st positional argument; a string URL;
                                          callers pass a full URL of a proxy
                                          document on another origin, ending in
                                          ".html"
  sysend.isPrimary()                no arguments
  sysend.id                         property read
  sysend.channel(origin)            optional member: callers guard with
                                    `typeof sysend.channel === '"'"'function'"'"'` and
                                    invoke it only when present
                                    origin — 1st positional argument; a string, a
                                          URL origin of the form scheme://host:port
                                    Both sides of the cross-origin relay make this
                                    probe: the app page passes the partner origin
                                    and the partner'"'"'s proxy document passes the
                                    app origin.

## Setup required
  - The app tree must live at `/app` and be servable as static files over plain
    HTTP. The calling side verifies that the `http://localhost:8080` origin serves
    the exact current bytes of `/app/sysend.js` and `/app/demo.html` and starts or
    replaces the serving process itself, so edits must be made in `/app` itself;
    a stale copy served from elsewhere cannot satisfy the callers.
  - Initialization happens at script load: every page that loads sysend.js joins
    the origin-wide channel (BroadcastChannel where available, localStorage-based
    fallback otherwise) with no explicit init call.
  - Framing between a page and an embedded proxy document is a JSON string
    carried by window postMessage: an object with `name`, `key`, and `data`
    members, where `name` equals the library'"'"'s internal message-prefix constant
    (defined by the library itself), `key` carries a channel event name, and
    `data` carries a payload. An instance that detects it runs inside an iframe
    registers the receiving window `message` listener; a parent page sends to its
    embedded proxy iframes using the same framing.
  - Cross-origin runs are genuine: the app origin and the partner/foreign origin
    differ in scheme//host (the foreign side is served from the container'"'"'s
    non-loopback address on a separate port), and foreign documents load the
    library from the app origin through an absolute script URL.

## Test harness boundary
  - A pytest runner drives a headless Chromium through the Playwright sync API,
    reaching the pages by evaluating inline JavaScript with `page.evaluate`.
    Chromium and Playwright are preinstalled image dependencies.
  - HTTP serving on the calling side: a session-scoped fixture guarantees that
    `localhost:8080` faithfully serves the current `/app` tree (the container
    entrypoint also runs `python3 -m http.server 8080` from `/app`; the fixture
    verifies or replaces that listener), and module-scoped fixtures run static
    file servers on kernel-assigned ports for the `/app` tree and for foreign
    staging sites under `/tmp`.
  - The calling side writes its own fixture pages at runtime; they are not part
    of the implementation: a victim page at `/app/victim_test_page.html`
    (rewritten per suite, overwriting whatever is there) and the partner/foreign
    documents staged in directories under `/tmp`. Callers create these files; the
    implementation must not ship or rebuild them.
