## Reached at
  An executable at the absolute path `/app/sqlcipher`, exec'"'"'d directly by the
  callers (no PATH lookup, no install step). It is the in-tree build artifact of
  the project source tree rooted at `/app` (the image build clones the SQLCipher
  v4.4.2 tree into `/app`, which is also the working directory, and builds it
  there). Two invocation shapes are used:
      /app/sqlcipher <database> <sql>        (SQL passed as a command-line argument)
      /app/sqlcipher <database>              (SQL fed on stdin as a script)
  `<database>` is a string: a filesystem path or `:memory:`.

  Through that binary, callers reach a SQL scalar function named
  `sqlcipher_export` by executing SQL text:
      SELECT sqlcipher_export(<target>);
      SELECT sqlcipher_export(<target>, <source>);
  In the existing tree the registration is per database connection (compiled in
  only when the codec is enabled): `src/func.c` registers the name with argument
  count -1 inside `sqlite3RegisterPerConnectionBuiltinFunctions`, bound to the
  existing implementation symbol `sqlcipher_exportFunc` (`src/crypto.c`, declared
  in `src/crypto.h`). Because registration is per connection, the name resolves
  on every connection the shell opens — argv mode and stdin script mode,
  `:memory:` or an on-disk database, before and after `ATTACH DATABASE`. It is
  not a loadable extension and not a separate executable.

## Must provide
  /app/sqlcipher       executable at this exact path, produced by the project'"'"'s
                       own build in /app (Makefile target `sqlcipher$(TEXE)` from
                       the generated `shell.c` and the compiled library)
  sqlcipher_export     SQL scalar function, callable in SELECT with one or two
                       arguments, registered on each connection the shell opens
  SQL script surface   multi-statement, `;`-separated SQL accepted both as the
                       second command-line argument and on stdin
  ATTACH DATABASE      an attach statement naming a file path under an alias is
                       used to set up the databases the export call then names

## Called as
  Command line, argument mode:
      /app/sqlcipher <database> <sql>
        1st positional argument: database — a string (a file path or `:memory:`)
        2nd positional argument: sql — a string holding one or more
                                 `;`-separated SQL statements

  Command line, script mode:
      /app/sqlcipher <database>            with the SQL text fed on stdin
        1st positional argument: database — as above
        stdin: a string holding one or more `;`-separated SQL statements

  sqlcipher_export(<target>):
      target  — 1st SQL argument; a SQL expression evaluated to the name of the
                target database

  sqlcipher_export(<target>, <source>):
      target  — 1st SQL argument; as above
      source  — 2nd SQL argument; a SQL expression evaluated to the name of the
                source database

  Caller-side script shape used around the export call (aliases and paths are
  the caller'"'"'s own):
      ATTACH DATABASE '"'"'<destination-file-path>'"'"' AS <alias>;
      SELECT sqlcipher_export('"'"'<alias>'"'"');
  and, for the two-argument form, with two attached databases named in the call.

## Setup required
  - The binary must exist at `/app/sqlcipher` as the in-tree build product of the
    project tree at `/app`. The image build that establishes the tree runs:
        ./configure --enable-tempstore=yes \
            CFLAGS="-DSQLITE_HAS_CODEC" LDFLAGS="-lcrypto"
        make
    and callers exec that in-tree binary; there is no `make install` in the path.
  - The `sqlcipher_export` registration is compiled in only when the codec is
    enabled (`SQLITE_HAS_CODEC`); the build flags above carry that definition,
    and callers identify the binary as a codec-enabled SQLCipher build (rather
    than a plain SQLite shell) via `PRAGMA cipher_version`.
  - OpenSSL libcrypto is a runtime link dependency of the binary (`-lcrypto`).
  - No particular working directory is required: callers use the absolute binary
    path and their own absolute or temporary database file paths.

## Test harness boundary
  A pytest runner drives everything by spawning `/app/sqlcipher` as a subprocess
  — once per statement batch through command-line arguments, and in script mode
  by writing the SQL text to the child'"'"'s stdin — and by capturing the child'"'"'s
  stdout and stderr. The calling side also creates and removes its own temporary
  database files and directories, opens a source database in one process, attaches
  a destination file and runs the export call, then opens the destination file in
  a separate fresh process for read-back. All of that — the runner, the
  subprocess plumbing, the temporary files — belongs to the calling side and is
  not part of the implementation; the implementation is the binary at
  `/app/sqlcipher` and the SQL surface it registers.
