## 5. Required Interface Reachability Contract

This block states the required interface identity, resolution, invocation, input shape, and setup that the implementation must realize. It does not state expected outcomes, prescribe private implementation structure, or expand edit scope.

```text
test_interface = import-by-name (signals: ['"'"'composer-autoload'"'"', '"'"'filesystem-artifact'"'"', '"'"'staged-input-files'"'"', '"'"'subprocess-harness'"'"'])

This block states only where and how existing callers reach the implementation.
It does not state expected outcomes or prescribe implementation structure.

## Reached at
  The implementation is the Smarty template-engine package `smarty/smarty`,
  installed by composer (locked at v4.1.0) at image build time; its source
  tree lives under /app/vendor/smarty/smarty. Callers resolve it through the
  generated composer autoloader and instantiate its top-level class. Runtime
  is the PHP CLI (/usr/local/bin/php, php:8.1-cli); working directory /app:

      require '"'"'/app/vendor/autoload.php'"'"';
      $smarty = new Smarty();

  The embedded-script caller form uses a path-relative require with cwd /app:

      require '"'"'vendor/autoload.php'"'"';

  The retained application entry point /app/index.php uses this same form.
  The image build copies the composer metadata into /app and runs
  `composer install --no-dev --no-interaction`. The PHP runtime and composer
  are pre-existing prerequisites; the package tree under /app/vendor/smarty/smarty
  is the owned code the caller-side execution lands in.

## Must provide
  /app/vendor/autoload.php    composer autoloader entry; after it is required,
                              `class_exists("Smarty")` resolves
  Smarty                      class, instantiable with no constructor arguments
  Smarty::setTemplateDir      public instance method, one string argument
  Smarty::setCompileDir       public instance method, one string argument
  Smarty::setCacheDir         public instance method, one string argument
  Smarty::setConfigDir        public instance method, one string argument
  Smarty::setForceCompile     public instance method, one boolean argument
  Smarty::assign              public instance method, a name and a value
  Smarty::configLoad          public instance method, one string argument
  Smarty::display             public instance method, one string argument
  Template-language surface reached through display(): {block name="..."} ... {/block};
  {include file="..."} with an optional inline attribute; {function name="..."} ...
  {/function} together with {call name="..."}; {if} / {else}; {foreach};
  {$var} variable display; {#config_var#} references bound via configLoad.
  Compiled artifacts          templates compile to .php files placed in the
                              configured compile directory; callers read those
                              files back after a run and tokenize them with the
                              PHP runtime'"'"'s own tokenizer

## Called as
  Embedded-script form (an ephemeral PHP script run as `php <script>` with
  cwd /app):

      $smarty = new Smarty();
      $smarty->setTemplateDir('"'"'./template'"'"');
      $smarty->setCompileDir('"'"'./compile'"'"');
      $smarty->setCacheDir('"'"'./cache'"'"');
      $smarty->assign('"'"'name'"'"', <value>);
      $smarty->display('"'"'<template file name>'"'"');

  assign — 1st argument: a string variable name; 2nd argument: a string, an
  integer, a boolean, or a list of strings. display — 1st argument: a string
  naming a template file present under the template directory.

  Driver form: a harness-supplied PHP driver script (fixed absolute path) is
  run as `php <driver>` with one JSON job object on stdin. Callers pass jobs
  of this shape:

      {
        "templates": {"<file name>": "<template source text>", ...},
        "configs":   {"<file name>": "<config source text>", ...},
        "config_load": "<config file name>",
        "display":   "<template file name>"
      }

  templates / configs — object mapping a file-name string to a source-text
  string; both optional. config_load — one string, optional. display — one
  string, required. Template sources passed by callers contain constructs of
  these shapes: {block name="<string>"}<body>{/block} (the name attribute is
  a double-quoted string; the body is template text); {include file="<string>"}
  and {include file="<string>" inline} (the file attribute is a double-quoted
  string; callers pass both bare file names and resource-prefixed
  specifications, including ones beginning `string:`); {function name="<string>"}
  <body>{/function} together with {call name="<string>"}; {#<key>#} config
  references. Config sources are lines of the form `<key> = <value>`.

  Inside the driver, each job becomes these Smarty calls (defaults shown; the
  driver creates any missing directory first):

      $smarty->setTemplateDir('"'"'/app/template'"'"');
      $smarty->setCompileDir('"'"'/app/compile'"'"');
      $smarty->setCacheDir('"'"'/app/cache'"'"');
      $smarty->setConfigDir('"'"'/app/config'"'"');
      $smarty->setForceCompile(true);
      $smarty->configLoad('"'"'<config file name>'"'"');   // when config_load present
      $smarty->display('"'"'<template file name>'"'"');

## Setup required
  - PHP CLI present at /usr/local/bin/php; the package installed under
    /app/vendor by composer at image build (the composer metadata locks
    smarty/smarty at v4.1.0).
  - Working directory /app for the relative-require caller form. The
    directories /app/template, /app/compile, /app/cache and /app/config must
    exist or be creatable before display runs.
  - Ordering: the caller side writes template files into /app/template and
    config files into /app/config itself, before the driver job runs; the
    driver checks the staged files against the job'"'"'s declared contents
    byte-for-byte and stops before reaching the engine on any mismatch, so a
    job'"'"'s `templates` and `configs` maps describe files that must already be
    on disk.
  - The driver sets force compile before display; each run therefore writes
    its own compiled files into the compile directory, and callers identify
    this run'"'"'s artifacts by diffing the compile directory before and after.

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side provides all of the following; none of it is part of the
  implementation and none of it is to be rebuilt:
  - a pytest runner that orchestrates the runs, writes the template and config
    input files under /app/template and /app/config itself, and launches PHP
    through subprocess;
  - an ephemeral PHP script the runner writes into /app, runs with cwd /app,
    and deletes after each run; it requires the autoloader and calls the
    Smarty API directly;
  - a JSON-job PHP driver script supplied with the harness assets, run as
    `php <driver>` with the job on stdin and a single JSON object on stdout;
    it does the staged-file checking, output buffering around display(), and
    compile-directory diffing described above;
  - a PHP analyzer script that tokenizes the compiled .php artifacts with the
    runtime'"'"'s own tokenizer and reports, for a given text fragment, which
    token categories the fragment appears in.
  These are means of reaching the implementation. The implementation itself is
  the composer-installed template-engine package under /app/vendor/smarty/smarty.
```

