test_interface = http/service (signals: ['"'"'bearer-jwt-auth'"'"', '"'"'developer-api-key-auth'"'"', '"'"'express-router-mount'"'"', '"'"'http-api'"'"', '"'"'json-body'"'"', '"'"'model-module-require'"'"', '"'"'readiness-probe'"'"'])

This block states only where and how existing callers reach the implementation.
It does not state expected outcomes or prescribe implementation structure.

## Reached at
  The callers drive a running HTTP service; base URL and route prefix:

      http://127.0.0.1:3001/api

  The service is the project'"'"'s own Express app, staged under /app by the
  image build. /app/server/index.js constructs the app, mounts an
  express.Router under the /api prefix and registers all route modules on
  that router, then listens on `process.env.SERVER_PORT || 3001`:

      app.use("/api", apiRouter);
      systemEndpoints(apiRouter);   // endpoints/system.js
      adminEndpoints(apiRouter);    // endpoints/admin.js
      developerEndpoints(app, apiRouter);   // endpoints/api/**

  The process runs with working directory /app/server and is started as
  `node index.js` (the image entrypoint keeps it in a restart loop).

  The user-update behavior the routes resolve lives in one project-owned
  CommonJS module, /app/server/models/user.js, exported as
  `module.exports = { User }`. Every caller of the update behavior requires
  it by path:

      const { User } = require("../models/user");

  with call sites in endpoints/admin.js, endpoints/system.js,
  endpoints/api/admin/index.js, and utils/PasswordRecovery/index.js.

## Must provide
  HTTP routes, all under the /api prefix of the app above:

      GET    /api/ping
      POST   /api/request-token
      POST   /api/system/enable-multi-user
      GET    /api/system/check-token
      POST   /api/system/user
      POST   /api/system/recover-account
      POST   /api/system/reset-password
      GET    /api/admin/users
      POST   /api/admin/users/new
      POST   /api/admin/user/:id
      DELETE /api/admin/user/:id
      POST   /api/admin/generate-api-key
      POST   /api/v1/admin/users/:id

  Module /app/server/models/user.js, export `User` (a plain object):

      User.update    async member function, called as User.update(userId, updates)
      User.create    async member function, called as User.create(params)
      User.get       async member function, called as User.get(clause)
      User.where     async member function, called as User.where()
      User.delete    async member function, called as User.delete(clause)

## Called as
  HTTP entry points, inputs as the existing callers issue them:

  POST /api/admin/user/:id
      Authorization: Bearer <session JWT of an admin or manager user>
      :id    URL path segment; a string
      body   JSON object; arbitrary string keys, values are JSON scalars
             (callers pass strings and numbers). The route reads the whole
             parsed JSON body and passes it as the `updates` argument of
             `User.update`.

  POST /api/v1/admin/users/:id
      Authorization: Bearer <developer API key>
      :id and body shaped exactly as above; the parsed body is likewise
      passed to `User.update` as `updates`.

  POST /api/system/user
      Authorization: Bearer <the user'"'"'s own session JWT>
      body   JSON object with the key `username` and/or the key `password`;
             values are strings

  POST /api/request-token
      body   JSON object { username, password }; values are strings

  POST /api/system/enable-multi-user
      body   JSON object { username, password }; values are strings

  GET  /api/admin/users
      Authorization: Bearer <session JWT of an admin or manager user>

  POST /api/admin/users/new
      Authorization: Bearer <session JWT>
      body   JSON object { username, password, role }; values are strings

  DELETE /api/admin/user/:id
      Authorization: Bearer <session JWT>; :id is a URL path segment

  POST /api/admin/generate-api-key
      Authorization: Bearer <session JWT>; no body

  GET  /api/system/check-token
      Authorization: Bearer <session JWT>

  POST /api/system/recover-account
      body   JSON object { username, recoveryCodes }; username is a string,
             recoveryCodes is a list of strings

  POST /api/system/reset-password
      body   JSON object { token, newPassword, confirmPassword }; values are
             strings

  GET  /api/ping
      no auth header, no body

  Model-layer call, as written at the route call sites:

      const { id } = request.params;
      const updates = reqBody(request);      // the parsed JSON body object
      await User.update(id, updates);

      userId    1st positional argument; a string (the URL path segment)
      updates   2nd positional argument; a plain object with arbitrary
                string keys

## Setup required
  - The service must already be up on 127.0.0.1:3001; callers probe
    readiness with GET /api/ping before issuing other requests. The image
    entrypoint starts it with `node index.js`, working directory
    /app/server, inside a restart loop that picks up code changes on
    restart.
  - Environment set by the image: SERVER_PORT=3001, NODE_ENV=production,
    JWT_SECRET set (session bearer tokens are HS256 JWTs keyed by it),
    STORAGE_DIR=/app/server/storage.
  - Persistence is a SQLite database at /app/server/storage/anythingllm.db
    reached through the generated Prisma client
    (`require("../utils/prisma")` -> @prisma/client); the schema and
    migrations live in /app/server/prisma and were applied at image build.
  - Multi-user mode is a runtime state of the app itself (its own system
    settings) that gates the /api/admin/* and /api/v1/admin/* surfaces; the
    callers put the app into that state through
    POST /api/system/enable-multi-user before using those surfaces.
  - The /api/admin/* surface authenticates session JWTs issued through
    POST /api/request-token; the /api/v1/admin/* surface authenticates
    developer API keys issued through POST /api/admin/generate-api-key.

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side is a pytest run (installed and launched through uv) that
  acts purely as an HTTP client against 127.0.0.1:3001 using the Python
  `requests` library. Its own fixtures poll the readiness route, put the app
  into multi-user mode through the routes above, and create and read back
  user records through those same routes. That runner, its fixtures, and its
  HTTP client belong to the caller and are not part of the implementation.
  The image entrypoint restart loop and the build-time prerequisites
  (node_modules, the generated Prisma client, the applied migrations) are
  infrastructure as well. What is owned and rebuilt is the project source
  under /app/server.
